= Feedback =

== Overview ==
'''Feedback''' occurs when information about a system's output or state is returned into the system and influences subsequent behavior.

Feedback is central to control theory, cybernetics, biology, engineering, and many complex systems.

== Closed loop ==
A feedback structure can be represented as:

<pre>
system → output/state
   ↑         ↓
   └── feedback ──
</pre>

The returned signal affects later inputs, control actions, or internal state.

== Negative feedback ==
Negative feedback tends to oppose deviations from a reference, equilibrium, or target condition.

It can stabilize systems, though delay or excessive gain can also produce oscillation or instability.

“Negative” describes the loop effect, not whether the result is bad.

== Positive feedback ==
Positive feedback reinforces deviations or changes.

It can generate growth, amplification, cascades, tipping behavior, or rapid transitions.

“Positive” does not mean beneficial.

== Feedback and response ==
A system responding to an input is not automatically feedback.

Feedback specifically requires that consequences of prior system behavior influence later behavior through a loop.

